    "body": "Cross-pollination play-test gift from the SuperInstance fleet (lane 55-d, task 55-d): a small, verified repair proven by the repo's own CLI + suite, before/after. Merge decision is entirely yours.\n\n## Root cause\n\nEach CLI invocation is a fresh process, but `main()` keeps `init` state only in **module memory** (`main.state`, set in the `init` branch and never persisted). So the `test-killer` branch's guard\n\n```python\nif not hasattr(main, \"state\") or main.state[\"dispatcher\"] is None:\n    print(\"Initialize first with 'cellforge init'\")\n    return 1\n```\n\ncan never be satisfied by any shell sequence: `cellforge init` exits before any state could survive, so `cellforge test-killer` **always** prints `Initialize first with 'cellforge init'` and exits 1. Meanwhile `cmd_killer_demo()` explicitly implements a self-contained path for exactly this case (`d is None` → scaffold `Workbook(\"killer-demo\")` + fresh `Dispatcher`) — dead code, unreachable through `main()`.\n\n## The fix (1 file, +8/−4)\n\n`src/cellforge/__main__.py`: when no live dispatcher state exists, pass a blank state into `cmd_killer_demo()` so its existing self-contained branch runs, instead of failing at the door. Behavior with a pre-existing `main.state` (e.g. embedded/in-process use) is unchanged. `cellforge status` is intentionally left as-is — real init-state persistence is a design decision (state file? location? serialization of cells?) that belongs to the owner; the play-test receipt notes it rather than smuggling a design in.\n\n## Before/after receipts (Python 3.12.14, Linux x86_64)\n\n| Command | Before (main @ 1a475f7) | After (this branch @ c6c0ff9) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PYTHONPATH=src python3 -m cellforge test-killer` | **exit 1** — `Initialize first with 'cellforge init'` | **exit 0** — `[OK] PERFECT PAUSE. Within 14ms, on the same tick, all workers halted.` — 100/100 workers acked, `ack_drift_ticks: 0`, `ack_latency_ms: 1.045` |\n| `python3 run_tests.py` | 42/42 passed, exit 0 | 42/42 passed, exit 0 (suite untouched) |\n\nThe after run is the killer demo doing what the README advertises: `[killer demo] registering 100 workers...` → tick 50 → pause → every worker halts on the same tick, sub-2ms.\n\n## Scope / honesty notes\n\n- Diff: 1 file, +8/−4. No test files touched; 42/42 still green.\n- No CI configured on this repo, so receipts are local-run only (commands + exit codes above are exactly reproducible on a fresh clone).\n- Related finding, receipted but NOT fixed here: `cellforge init` → `cellforge status` across two shell invocations also can't work (same in-memory-state root cause; status exits 1 with a misleading \"Run 'cellforge init' first\" hint). That needs a persistence design decision, so it is left to the owner.\n- Cosmetic, for the backlog: `pyproject.toml` says `version = \"0.1.0\"` while the README ships v0.3.0 notes.\n\n— fleet lane 55-d, play-test + gift lane",
